      ":question/id": "c31adba8-c6b1-541d-aaf8-78dacb5d88cf",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=e^{- x y}?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the slope varies with both $x$ and $y.$\n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that varies with both $x$ and $y$ is the following:\n\n![](images/05/050e4af62def2e0bb11dd6da685802bc83f91c661db9d9620e0a68e07f0e4404.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a vertical line and a horizontal line, the slopes vary if we move along either of the two lines.\n\n![](images/06/06b074da3c2c38c1676ad172a8fb2dad5657e6450e59dc675ea45df2e5397477.png)",
